WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:06.006
Sustainable investing is
sort of an umbrella term.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.591
It's been a breakout
year for Environmental,

00:00:08.591 --> 00:00:11.970
Social and Governance
issues, known better as ESG.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.306
2020 was really an inflection for ESG

00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:18.685
and as a result, it's
become a mainstream topic.

00:00:18.685 --> 00:00:22.981
That kind of subgroup
called ESG is really by far

00:00:22.981 --> 00:00:25.859
the biggest area within
sustainable investing,

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:30.238
which in just the past few
years has gone from nothing

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:34.117
to more than $30 trillion
in assets under management.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:38.913
Sustainability, it's more
than an environmental issue.

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:41.916
As everybody seeks to
sort of invest their money

00:00:41.916 --> 00:00:43.877
in a way that they believe, at least,

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:46.421
is more aligned with their values.

00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:48.131
I believe there will be a future

00:00:48.131 --> 00:00:49.966
that all investments are going

00:00:49.966 --> 00:00:51.801
to be looked through sustainability.

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:54.345
That's why I really talk to our people

00:00:54.345 --> 00:00:55.430
about just four words,

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:57.182
higher returns, lower carbon.

00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:00.018
The key question that we've
been looking at this year

00:01:00.018 --> 00:01:03.772
is how much is sustainable
investing helping the world,

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:06.357
making capitalism more sustainable

00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:08.359
and how much is it making
money for Wall Street?

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:10.070
The Securities and Exchange Commission

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.030
has come up with a regulatory agenda

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:13.698
to bring in more regulation

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:16.576
as far as ESG disclosures are concerned.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.953
You've got to pull back the top layers

00:01:18.953 --> 00:01:20.747
and look more deeply.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.665
There is a threat of greenwashing

00:01:22.665 --> 00:01:26.086
and it needs to be monitored,
it needs to be dealt with.

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:27.128
In that process,

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:28.546
we decided that we wanted to look back

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:31.091
at the origins of sustainable investing.

00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:34.010
I found a book that was published in 1991,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.889
dedicated in part to these
two obscure financiers

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:40.642
called Robert Schwartz and Robert Zevin.

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:44.062
I set out to find out as much as I could

00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:45.355
about these two men,

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:46.606
their investment practices,

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.316
how they were viewed on Wall Street

00:01:48.316 --> 00:01:51.569
and also to find them if I possibly could.

00:02:01.162 --> 00:02:03.832
Robert Schwartz died in 2006,

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:07.168
but I did find an extraordinary
documentary decades old

00:02:07.168 --> 00:02:08.378
but I did find an extraordinary
documentary decades old

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:12.215
that had been made about him,
innovating these practices

00:02:12.215 --> 00:02:15.927
by a filmmaker who spent
a ton of time with him

00:02:15.927 --> 00:02:19.305
and followed him around
across the country.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.766
Well, I didn't grow up intending to be

00:02:21.766 --> 00:02:25.395
an investment advisor any
more than I did a fireman

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.396
or a policeman.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:32.193
Business Not As Usual is an 18 minute film

00:02:32.193 --> 00:02:33.820
that I

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:38.867
recorded on 16mm film

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:40.869
when films were really films

00:02:41.744 --> 00:02:46.291
in the 1980s and the 1990s.

00:02:46.291 --> 00:02:47.876
At that time,

00:02:47.876 --> 00:02:50.670
I was considerably younger than I am now

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.673
and I came upon people

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.843
who I discovered were

00:02:56.843 --> 00:03:00.388
using their work in ways
that I had never heard of.

00:03:00.388 --> 00:03:03.474
Twenty-five years ago I
realized that a strategy

00:03:03.474 --> 00:03:04.976
of directing investments,

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:08.021
according to progressive
values could become a forceful

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:10.356
instrument for social change.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:12.692
Robert Schwartz grew up
during The Depression.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.985
The world's richest

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:16.738
and supposedly soundest economy perished

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:19.908
and small business was strangled
by The Great Depression.

00:03:19.908 --> 00:03:22.410
He was born in 1917,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:27.916
so he was coming of age during
the time when America was

00:03:27.916 --> 00:03:31.336
really at its lowest financially

00:03:31.336 --> 00:03:33.922
with millions of people unemployed,

00:03:33.922 --> 00:03:36.132
with millions of people going hungry.

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:38.635
This had a lifelong impact on him.

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:42.347
I did go through The
Depression as a teenager

00:03:42.347 --> 00:03:45.975
and keenly aware of
the problems of poverty

00:03:45.975 --> 00:03:46.851
and unemployment.

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:51.022
Robert Schwartz was a senior
level official at the U.S.

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:54.442
Treasury Department when
World War II began

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:56.819
and so he enrolled in the U.S. Marine Corps

00:03:56.819 --> 00:03:59.405
and he was sent to the Pacific.

00:04:00.907 --> 00:04:03.159
After the war he went back
to the Treasury Department

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:05.203
where he worked in the
international section.

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:08.164
We should remember that
practically every issue which we

00:04:08.164 --> 00:04:13.169
face today from high taxes
to the shameful mess in Korea

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.964
is inextricably interwoven

00:04:17.048 --> 00:04:18.675
with the communist issue.

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:20.468
Sadly after the war,

00:04:20.468 --> 00:04:24.264
there was also kind of a series
of witch-hunts that began

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:27.809
in the U.S. going after so-called communists

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:31.437
or leftists who were
inside the U.S. Government.

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:34.315
Apparently every time
anybody says anything

00:04:34.315 --> 00:04:37.360
against anybody working
for Senator McCarthy,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.030
he is accusing them of communism.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:43.408
It swept up Hollywood,
it swept up literature,

00:04:43.408 --> 00:04:45.368
it swept up Washington D.C.

00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:48.913
and the highest-powered
corners of the U.S. Government,

00:04:48.913 --> 00:04:51.874
especially the Treasury
Department and The Pentagon.

00:04:51.874 --> 00:04:54.544
This body is improperly constituted.

00:04:54.544 --> 00:04:58.423
It is a kangaroo court, it
does not have my respect.

00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:00.758
It has my upmost contempt.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:02.260
Robert Schwartz caught up in this

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:04.262
because he was a leftist.

00:05:04.262 --> 00:05:07.056
He was incredibly
progressive for his time

00:05:07.056 --> 00:05:08.808
and he had to give up his career

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:13.104
and his livelihood and the
future of his family even

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:15.440
was in jeopardy because
he didn't know how,

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.109
or if he would ever be able
to make a living again.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:21.863
It created a

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:25.033
lifelong desire on his part

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:29.537
to challenge established authorities.

00:05:29.537 --> 00:05:33.124
It also led to him wanting to,

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:37.920
in whatever ways that he
could, use the next steps

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.048
that were at that time unknown,

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:44.385
to again, pursue what he
believed strongly in.

00:05:44.385 --> 00:05:47.555
I was put in a position
of really having to retire

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:50.558
from the Treasury Department
and started life over again,

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:53.311
back in New York with
the Amalgamated Bank.

00:05:53.311 --> 00:05:55.938
He finally found a bank on Wall Street

00:05:55.938 --> 00:05:58.483
that was a pretty unique bank
and a pretty innovative bank,

00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:00.818
and also a pretty progressive bank.

00:06:00.818 --> 00:06:02.278
It was called The Amalgamated Bank

00:06:02.278 --> 00:06:05.114
and it was set up by a
textile workers union

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:09.410
that represented European
Jews who were fleeing Europe

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:10.828
in the 20th century

00:06:10.828 --> 00:06:13.748
and coming to work in one
of the toughest industries,

00:06:13.748 --> 00:06:16.292
which was the textile,
the garment industry

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:20.755
and the bank was founded by
the union as a safe repository

00:06:20.755 --> 00:06:23.508
for the workers earnings,
for their pensions,

00:06:23.508 --> 00:06:24.884
for their welfare funds.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.053
But also as a way to
provide credit to them

00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:29.931
so they could start the American dream

00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:33.267
and own a home and have access to finance

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:34.644
that could change their lives

00:06:34.644 --> 00:06:37.355
and the lives of their families.

00:06:40.108 --> 00:06:41.943
When Robert went into
the Amalgamated Bank,

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:43.736
he discovered something
pretty extraordinary.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:48.408
He was responsible for about
a billion dollars in assets

00:06:48.408 --> 00:06:51.369
that the bank had, which
is a huge amount back then

00:06:51.369 --> 00:06:54.288
and he discovered that a
significant amount of these funds

00:06:54.288 --> 00:06:58.167
were invested by a union essentially

00:06:58.167 --> 00:07:01.003
in companies that were anti-union

00:07:01.003 --> 00:07:02.422
that were breaking strikes,

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:04.757
that refused to negotiate
with their workers,

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:07.844
that were even engaged
in supporting violence

00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:10.138
against their own workers on picket lines.

00:07:10.138 --> 00:07:13.891
And he thought that the bank
should definitely alter its

00:07:13.891 --> 00:07:17.895
investment practices to
not support companies

00:07:17.895 --> 00:07:21.149
that were engaged in
unfair labor practices

00:07:21.149 --> 00:07:25.570
and this was really a first for anyone.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:29.907
Robert Schwartz believed
that it was possible

00:07:29.907 --> 00:07:32.827
both to do well

00:07:32.827 --> 00:07:34.412
and to do good.

00:07:34.412 --> 00:07:38.541
Portfolios for clients may
avoid military contractors,

00:07:38.541 --> 00:07:42.420
exclude nuclear power,
destroyers of the environment,

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:47.383
union-busters and
business in South Africa.

00:07:48.259 --> 00:07:51.679
To replace those companies,
I seek innovative companies

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:53.097
meeting social needs.

00:07:53.097 --> 00:07:57.185
He was not after maximum profits.

00:07:57.185 --> 00:08:03.858
He was after maximum
profits that would fit with

00:08:03.858 --> 00:08:04.942
what he believed

00:08:04.942 --> 00:08:08.446
and what most importantly,
his clients believed.

00:08:08.446 --> 00:08:11.240
Had his clients not believed in that,

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:15.620
my guess is that rather
than change his principles,

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:17.413
he would have dropped those clients.

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:21.584
While Robert Schwartz
was building his practice

00:08:21.584 --> 00:08:24.754
around these principles,
another investment advisor,

00:08:24.754 --> 00:08:26.047
another economist,

00:08:26.047 --> 00:08:30.426
this one trained at Harvard,
was building a very similar

00:08:30.426 --> 00:08:34.388
business for clients starting
with his family in Boston.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.728
I started investing for myself

00:08:40.728 --> 00:08:45.733
when my parents began giving
me stock in the company,

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:46.817
they encouraged me,

00:08:46.817 --> 00:08:49.028
my father anyway, encouraged me to sell it

00:08:49.028 --> 00:08:50.988
and invest it, which I did.

00:08:50.988 --> 00:08:53.241
I must have been extraordinarily lucky

00:08:53.241 --> 00:08:56.786
because I invested all my money
in only one or two stocks

00:08:56.786 --> 00:09:00.748
and I just had two or
three or four big winners.

00:09:00.748 --> 00:09:05.503
I said, "Great, so by the time I'm 30,
I'll be a millionaire

00:09:05.503 --> 00:09:06.796
just from investing."

00:09:07.672 --> 00:09:08.673
And did that happen?

00:09:08.673 --> 00:09:09.507
No.

00:09:11.425 --> 00:09:12.635
It did not happen.

00:09:16.389 --> 00:09:19.433
I started an organization called Resist,

00:09:19.433 --> 00:09:24.021
designed to deliver
support for individuals,

00:09:24.021 --> 00:09:29.610
mostly for civilians, who used
passive resistance, tax refusal,

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.821
whatever, to oppose the war

00:09:31.821 --> 00:09:34.448
and a year later I started

00:09:34.448 --> 00:09:36.909
the United States Servicemen's Fund,

00:09:36.909 --> 00:09:40.538
which had the more important ambition

00:09:40.538 --> 00:09:44.208
and more useful ambition
of supporting servicemen

00:09:44.208 --> 00:09:46.252
and women who opposed the war.

00:09:46.252 --> 00:09:49.380
So, in the day I'm teaching

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:52.717
in the weekends I'm investing

00:09:52.717 --> 00:09:53.884
and in the evenings,

00:09:53.884 --> 00:09:56.012
I'm knocking on people's doors

00:09:56.012 --> 00:10:01.017
and going and visiting them to
raise money for these causes.

00:10:01.017 --> 00:10:02.310
But a few of them would say,

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.438
"What, a person with your politics

00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:07.398
you're in the investment business?

00:10:07.398 --> 00:10:09.483
I'd love to have you manage my money."

00:10:09.483 --> 00:10:10.026
They would say,

00:10:10.026 --> 00:10:13.654
"But I don't wanna own
weapons manufacturers.

00:10:13.654 --> 00:10:17.241
I don't wanna own people who make napalm.

00:10:17.241 --> 00:10:19.869
I don't wanna own people
who do strip mining.

00:10:19.869 --> 00:10:22.121
I don't wanna own
companies in South Africa."

00:10:22.121 --> 00:10:24.206
So it was the investors really who brought

00:10:24.206 --> 00:10:25.041
the idea to you.

00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:27.251
It was the investors who
brought the idea to me

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:30.671
and I was very reluctant
and very skeptical

00:10:30.671 --> 00:10:35.426
and it took about five years
of success to convince me that

00:10:35.426 --> 00:10:39.805
I had been wrong and that
this didn't cost anything.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:44.018
More than 7,000 anti-Vietnam
War demonstrators

00:10:44.018 --> 00:10:45.686
parade near the Los Angeles hotel,

00:10:45.686 --> 00:10:47.021
where President Johnson attends

00:10:47.021 --> 00:10:49.106
a Democratic fundraising dinner.

00:10:49.106 --> 00:10:51.233
The civil rights movement
and the Vietnam War

00:10:51.233 --> 00:10:54.278
both created similar momentum

00:10:54.278 --> 00:10:57.239
for some really clever activists

00:10:57.239 --> 00:10:59.784
who realized that they could use money

00:10:59.784 --> 00:11:03.954
and boycotts specifically
to channel their activism

00:11:03.954 --> 00:11:07.208
into capital and to hold back capital

00:11:07.208 --> 00:11:09.710
or divest from companies that

00:11:09.710 --> 00:11:12.338
or other actors that weren't
supporting civil rights

00:11:12.338 --> 00:11:14.715
or were supporting the Vietnam War.

00:11:14.715 --> 00:11:19.804
Probably the first really
high profile war contractor

00:11:19.804 --> 00:11:23.891
that was targeted in at
least modern U.S. history

00:11:23.891 --> 00:11:25.559
was the Dow Chemical Company

00:11:25.559 --> 00:11:29.397
and the Dow Chemical Company
was manufacturing napalm

00:11:29.397 --> 00:11:33.317
for the U.S. military
for its use in Vietnam.

00:11:33.317 --> 00:11:39.156
Nearly 400,000 tons of napalm
were dropped on Vietnam

00:11:39.156 --> 00:11:44.120
it was estimated within a
decade from 1963 to 1973.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:45.705
So the Dow Chemical Company,

00:11:45.705 --> 00:11:48.374
which was making napalm
and profiting from napalm

00:11:48.374 --> 00:11:52.503
became a main target of the
investment activist community,

00:11:52.503 --> 00:11:55.297
which was really coming
together for the first time.

00:11:55.297 --> 00:11:58.759
The boycotts against Dow
did start to take a toll

00:11:58.759 --> 00:12:01.011
and they proved effective.

00:12:01.011 --> 00:12:04.682
Publicizing the destructive
role of military contractors,

00:12:04.682 --> 00:12:07.977
caused many on Wall
Street to oppose the war.

00:12:07.977 --> 00:12:10.938
This lent more legitimacy
to the peace movement

00:12:10.938 --> 00:12:12.565
and helped bring an end to the war,

00:12:12.565 --> 00:12:15.067
which claimed 50,000 American lives

00:12:15.067 --> 00:12:17.236
and over 1 million Vietnamese.

00:12:17.236 --> 00:12:18.320
After Dow Chemical,

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:20.072
the movement really started to grow

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:22.408
and it grew beyond labor standards

00:12:22.408 --> 00:12:25.536
and a main new target of activists

00:12:25.536 --> 00:12:30.332
and activist investors became
every major U.S. company

00:12:30.332 --> 00:12:32.877
that was doing business in South Africa.

00:12:32.877 --> 00:12:37.465
South Africa was run by
a white minority regime

00:12:37.465 --> 00:12:39.842
and was under attack and under pressure

00:12:39.842 --> 00:12:42.511
from virtually all corners of the world.

00:12:42.511 --> 00:12:45.514
And it was really the
activist community joining up

00:12:45.514 --> 00:12:48.142
with people like Robert
Schwartz and Robert Zevin,

00:12:48.142 --> 00:12:50.603
who started to push
the divestment movement

00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:52.146
in the United States.

00:12:52.146 --> 00:12:54.982
The exclusion of the
companies in South Africa,

00:12:54.982 --> 00:12:59.945
from the S&amp;P 500 has resulted
in better performance.

00:13:00.237 --> 00:13:04.533
Both Robert Schwartz and
Robert Zevin were surprised

00:13:04.533 --> 00:13:05.618
when they ran the numbers,

00:13:05.618 --> 00:13:09.997
their strategies of not
investing in companies that were

00:13:09.997 --> 00:13:12.082
doing things that they and their investors

00:13:12.082 --> 00:13:16.086
found horrible, was actually
working out incredibly well

00:13:16.086 --> 00:13:18.047
for their investors and for them.

00:13:18.047 --> 00:13:20.800
Do you remember if there
was a moment when you really

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:23.803
thought this could be a good thing?

00:13:23.803 --> 00:13:26.972
I'm not sure I remember
some light going off

00:13:26.972 --> 00:13:30.559
but years later, I did some research

00:13:30.559 --> 00:13:33.521
about the effects of these screens

00:13:33.521 --> 00:13:38.984
and proved that the restrictions
had no effect on results

00:13:38.984 --> 00:13:41.445
and many other people
did the same research

00:13:41.445 --> 00:13:43.739
but didn't tell anybody about it

00:13:43.739 --> 00:13:46.450
because they didn't want to
embarrass their clients

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:47.660
who were opposed to it.

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:51.080
Eliminating each whole sector

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.832
of the S&amp;P 500

00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:56.418
doesn't make any difference
over 5, 10, 15, 20 years,

00:13:56.418 --> 00:13:58.045
you still get the same results,

00:13:58.045 --> 00:14:00.589
which is kind of amazing
and counterintuitive.

00:14:00.589 --> 00:14:05.010
It's now a widely accepted idea
that all these restrictions

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.554
do not make any difference.

00:14:06.554 --> 00:14:10.432
Divestment should not be ruled
out on financial grounds.

00:14:10.432 --> 00:14:13.060
The final consideration is not its effect

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:15.312
on your investment portfolio,

00:14:15.312 --> 00:14:17.690
but its effect on apartheid.

00:14:19.191 --> 00:14:21.235
I think the most extraordinary
thing that I've found

00:14:21.235 --> 00:14:22.695
in looking at these two men,

00:14:22.695 --> 00:14:26.198
Robert Schwartz and Robert
Zevin was how they just flew in

00:14:26.198 --> 00:14:29.827
the face of everything that
was happening politically.

00:14:29.827 --> 00:14:32.079
This was the Reagan - Thatcher era.

00:14:32.079 --> 00:14:33.497
Everything that was
happening on Wall Street,

00:14:33.497 --> 00:14:35.499
everything that was happening
in the City of London

00:14:35.499 --> 00:14:40.421
was the exact opposite of what
these two peacenik economists

00:14:40.421 --> 00:14:42.715
were pushing as investment advisors

00:14:42.715 --> 00:14:46.844
and they were viewed
somewhat openly at times

00:14:46.844 --> 00:14:50.222
with hostility by other
investment advisors,

00:14:50.222 --> 00:14:52.892
by other people in the
business that they were in.

00:14:53.809 --> 00:14:55.269
Almost never did they say,

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:57.396
"Oh, that's very interesting."

00:14:57.396 --> 00:14:58.772
Almost always they said,

00:14:58.772 --> 00:15:01.734
"Oh, that's almost, that's impossible.

00:15:01.734 --> 00:15:03.068
That must be very difficult."

00:15:03.068 --> 00:15:05.863
Or "Why would you do that?"

00:15:05.863 --> 00:15:08.699
It always seemed like a
foreign land to me anyway

00:15:08.699 --> 00:15:12.912
so the impact on me was nil basically.

00:15:12.912 --> 00:15:16.707
I just kept plodding along on my own path.

00:15:16.707 --> 00:15:19.460
The practice of social investment that I

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:24.298
and a few others began 25 years ago 
has garnered increasing

00:15:24.298 --> 00:15:27.718
acceptance and respect with
the huge amounts of money

00:15:27.718 --> 00:15:31.972
available for productive
application, investment decisions

00:15:31.972 --> 00:15:35.893
can profoundly shape public
discussion in social conditions.

00:15:35.893 --> 00:15:39.188
Socially responsible investing
is a powerful vehicle for

00:15:39.188 --> 00:15:42.566
pointing the way to a more
just and humane society.

00:15:43.943 --> 00:15:46.111
There is still a handful of niche funds

00:15:46.111 --> 00:15:51.116
and niche investment
firms that really focus on

00:15:51.116 --> 00:15:53.077
the kinds of principles
that Robert Schwartz

00:15:53.077 --> 00:15:54.995
and Robert Zevin put into practice.

00:15:54.995 --> 00:15:55.955
But in the past couple of years,

00:15:55.955 --> 00:15:59.291
what's really changed is
Wall Street has capitalized

00:15:59.291 --> 00:16:02.252
on this demand by investors

00:16:02.252 --> 00:16:04.046
to want to make the world a better place,

00:16:04.046 --> 00:16:06.507
but it's done it mostly through marketing

00:16:06.507 --> 00:16:10.552
and underneath that marketing
are some rather dubious claims

00:16:10.552 --> 00:16:13.764
about making the world a better place.

00:16:13.764 --> 00:16:17.768
We're seeing transformative
changes in almost every sector

00:16:17.768 --> 00:16:19.019
of the economy

00:16:19.019 --> 00:16:21.271
and that's very, very encouraging,

00:16:21.271 --> 00:16:25.067
but investors who want
to participate in this

00:16:25.067 --> 00:16:30.572
have to take care, to identify
the risk of greenwashing.

00:16:30.572 --> 00:16:34.326
Success was the mother of defeat somehow.

00:16:34.326 --> 00:16:37.079
I think some of the original pioneers

00:16:37.079 --> 00:16:39.331
were actually the instigators of

00:16:40.874 --> 00:16:43.210
deciding that it really wasn't socially

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:45.212
responsible investing anymore

00:16:45.212 --> 00:16:50.300
it was 'ESG' Environment, Social
and Governance investing,

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:53.345
which to my way of thinking,

00:16:53.345 --> 00:16:56.223
was taking something that
was a social movement

00:16:56.223 --> 00:16:58.726
and that was intimately
connected to people

00:16:58.726 --> 00:17:00.894
who weren't investors
and didn't own stock,

00:17:00.894 --> 00:17:03.439
but were struggling to make a living

00:17:03.439 --> 00:17:04.565
or to end a war

00:17:04.565 --> 00:17:06.275
or to escape from a war.

00:17:06.275 --> 00:17:08.527
It somehow threw all those people out

00:17:08.527 --> 00:17:09.153
and it said,

00:17:09.153 --> 00:17:11.238
"Now we're a niche product."

00:17:11.238 --> 00:17:13.574
It's not just Wall Street, it's capitalism,

00:17:13.574 --> 00:17:16.994
it always finds some way
to repackage an idea

00:17:16.994 --> 00:17:21.040
so that it's profitable
and mass-producible

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:24.084
and that's going to be hard to overcome.

00:17:26.795 --> 00:17:28.964
All of the really ground-breaking things

00:17:28.964 --> 00:17:32.760
that these two men did in
finance are really what led us to

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.304
this extraordinary moment today,

00:17:35.304 --> 00:17:38.223
where this has become a 
multi-trillion-dollar business

00:17:38.223 --> 00:17:39.266
on Wall Street.

00:17:40.476 --> 00:17:43.228
The problem for people
who've invested their money

00:17:43.228 --> 00:17:44.354
in these strategies

00:17:44.354 --> 00:17:47.399
and in these funds, especially
in the United States,

00:17:47.399 --> 00:17:51.904
is that fundamentally the
largest funds don't address the

00:17:51.904 --> 00:17:55.074
key questions that people
believe are being addressed

00:17:55.074 --> 00:17:56.075
by the funds.

00:17:56.075 --> 00:17:59.453
In fact, some of the biggest
ones actually can contribute

00:17:59.453 --> 00:18:02.081
to making things even worse.

